      <title>When we stop treating children like children</title>
      <link>https://www.dhakatribune.com/opinion/op-ed/425212/when-we-stop-treating-children-like-children</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.dhakatribune.com/opinion/op-ed/425212/when-we-stop-treating-children-like-children</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 02:53:55 +0600</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/20/73fc2f3e0fe0262b0eedb6546b2ce410-6aff61b533619.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=77889" length="0" type="image/jpeg" />
      <media:content url="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/20/73fc2f3e0fe0262b0eedb6546b2ce410-6aff61b533619.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=77889" medium="image" />
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/20/73fc2f3e0fe0262b0eedb6546b2ce410-6aff61b533619.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=77889" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;margin-bottom:10px;" alt="When we stop treating children like children" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;There is a familiar joke in South Asian families. A relative gives a child money as Eidi on Eid. The child is happy, counts the notes, and starts thinking about what to buy. Then comes the familiar instruction: “Give it to me. I will keep it safe for you.” And somehow, the money disappears.
We laugh&lt;/p&gt;</description>
